    {
        "content": "<p>Last  year, the article \"<em>A counterexample to the strong Skorokhod representation theorem</em>\" [1] was published. It shows that a certain 'theorem', which was purportedly proved in 2018, is (as quoted from the abstract)</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>'wrong and cannot be corrected'</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>by constructing a counterexample. This 'theorem' had already been applied in a few dozen papers, which are cited in the article.</p>\n<p>[1]. M. Ondreját, J. Seidler, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s40072-025-00357-0\">A counterexample to the strong Skorokhod representation theorem.</a> <em>Stoch PDE: Anal Comp</em> <strong>13</strong>, 1355–1360 (2025).</p>\n<p>Since the counterexample uses only elementary measure/probability theory, I thought it would be good target for formalization, and took it upon myself to give it a try. I have since succeeded to prove the statements from the paper (a sketch of the statements is written below).</p>\n<p>I would like to know whether this formalization would be a good fit for the <code>Counterexamples</code> folder in Mathlib. If so, I will clean up the proof (currently ~300 lines including some auxiliary lemmas) a bit more and make a PR.</p>\n<p>As a byproduct of this formalization, I also proved several statements about probability theory which were missing from mathlib. These will be PR'd separately.</p>\n<p>I also have a question: for the formalization to be correct, it is important that the right topologies are used for <code>U</code>, <code>V</code> and <code>ProbabilityMeasure U × V</code>. Currently it seems that typeclass synthesis indeed gives the right topologies. But is there a good way to enforce this in the theorem statement itself? </p>\n<p>Sketch of the formalized statements:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- We assume we are given some probability measure ρ on V = ℝ.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Some notation and objects needed for the construction</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">θ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">infinitePi</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">U</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">U</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- The sequence of measures which forms the counterexample</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">U</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">θ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">map</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Helper to convert `Measure` to `ProbabilityMeasure`</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MeasureTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">inferInstance</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Fact 1: μ n converges weakly to θ × ρ</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tendsto_μ_θ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Tendsto</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">atTop</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nhds</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">((</span><span class=\"n\">θ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Fact 2: If there exist random variables A' and B' n on a probability space Ω', such that</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- (A', B' n) has law μ n and B' n converges almost surely, then ρ must be a Dirac measure.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">measure_eq_dirac_of_strong_skorokhod</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">Ω'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MeasurableSpace</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω'</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">P'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Measure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω'</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">IsProbabilityMeasure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P'</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">A'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">U</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">B'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">B'_lim</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ω'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h_law</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">HasLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω'</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω'</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">μ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h_tt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀ᵐ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∂</span><span class=\"n\">P'</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Tendsto</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω'</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">atTop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">nhds</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">B'_lim</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ω'</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">V</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ρ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">dirac</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Combining Facts 1 and 2 shows that the conclusion of the</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- 'strong Skorokhod representation theorem' cannot hold in general.</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Regarding use of AI: before I started formalizing, I fed the paper into Aristotle and asked it to autoformalize it. It succeeded (more or less) after one retry, of course with an unusable proof. The final version of the proof is however completely handwritten and even uses a slightly different and more efficient argument than the one from the paper.</p>",
        "id": 595252874,
        "sender_full_name": "Joris van Winden",
        "timestamp": 1778834475
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>IMO it can depend on specifics but often if you want the statement itself to use a specific instance , you may pass it in as an argument using “@“ ( as it’s often unnamed ) , in the right places. </p>\n<p>Another way though is to use “letI : (the specific structure you need as your instance) := (the actual proof often just using inferInstance);” before your statement (i.e still before the ‘:=’ but after  ‘:’) which forces the synthesis to pick those to use in the statement ( as it looks in local context first afaik).</p>\n<p>And there is also the option to control the instance via your imports and via <code>omit</code> .</p>",
        "id": 595283868,
        "sender_full_name": "Yan Yablonovskiy 🇺🇦",
        "timestamp": 1778845161
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Using <code>@</code> is a bit inconvenient because the chain of instances is somewhat long (from <code>TopologicalSpace U</code> + <code>TopologicalSpace V</code> to <code>TopologicalSpace U × V</code> to <code>TopologicalSpace (ProbabilityMeasure (U × V))</code>. But I like the approach of using <code>letI</code>, thanks!</p>",
        "id": 595372155,
        "sender_full_name": "Joris van Winden",
        "timestamp": 1778869963
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think you might also just add an <code>example : &lt;your desired instance&gt; = inferInstance := rfl</code> to show the inferred instance indeed agree with you desired instance.</p>",
        "id": 595423651,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1778890618
    },
    {
        "content": "<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39501\">#39501</a> (tightness of marginals)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39503\">#39503</a> (api for product measures)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39517\">#39517</a> (ae convergence implies convergence in distribution)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39543\">#39543</a> (portmanteau condition for compact sets)</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 596095130,
        "sender_full_name": "Joris van Winden",
        "timestamp": 1779179551
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/39588\">#39588</a> introduces <code>Measure.toProbabilityMeasure</code>, allowing use of dot notation to convert from <code>Measure</code> to <code>ProbabilityMeasure</code>. Perhaps this PR warrants some discussion.</p>",
